{"version":"6.9","hitCount":35718,"nextCursorMark":"AoJwgJ+Mt6MDKDU2MTQ2OTk2","nextPageUrl":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/search?query=%28alphafold%29&cursorMark=AoJwgJ%2BMt6MDKDU2MTQ2OTk2&resultType=core&pageSize=3&sort=P_PDATE_D+desc&format=json","request":{"queryString":"(alphafold)","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":3,"sort":"P_PDATE_D desc","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"42902760","source":"MED","pmid":"42902760","pmcid":"PMC13690281","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13690281"]},"doi":"10.1080/19336934.2026.2749209","title":"From bench to farm: leveraging &lt;i&gt;Drosophila&lt;/i&gt; as a model insect for agricultural pest management.","authorString":"Cai YD, Chiu JC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Cai YD","lastName":"Cai","firstName":"Yao D","initials":"YD","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3571-2978"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Entomology and Nematology, College of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences, University of California Davis, Davis, CA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Chiu JC","lastName":"Chiu","firstName":"Joanna C","initials":"JC","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7613-8127"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Entomology and Nematology, College of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences, University of California Davis, Davis, CA, USA."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7613-8127"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3571-2978"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"21","journalIssueId":4296145,"dateOfPublication":"2027 Dec","monthOfPublication":12,"yearOfPublication":2027,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Fly (Austin)","title":"Fly","essn":"1933-6942","issn":"1933-6934","isoabbreviation":"Fly (Austin)","nlmid":"101470897"},"printPublicationDate":"2027-12-01"},"pubYear":"2027","pageInfo":"2749209","abstractText":"The vinegar fly, <i>Drosophila melanogaster</i>, has contributed tremendously to advancing our understanding of the fundamentals of biology, from the chromosomal theory of inheritance to the survival of multi-cellular organisms in spaceflight. The use of this insect as a model organism has provided insights into all branches of life sciences, including agricultural and environmental sciences. In the context of integrated pest management (IPM), which often relies on species-specific natural history and phenology of pest insects and biological control, <i>D. melanogaster</i> flies have continued to be a powerhouse. In this review, we seek to summarize the use of the <i>D. melanogaster</i> model in studying insect biology relevant to IPM, with a focus on investigating the mechanisms underlying insecticide resistance. We sample case studies that adopt forward genetic approaches to reveal molecular underpinnings of IPM-relevant phenotypes and reverse genetic approaches that elucidate genotype-phenotype relationships. In particular, we highlight the contribution of <i>D. melanogaster</i> in understanding the biology and in developing IPM for the spotted-wing drosophila, also known as <i>D. suzukii</i>, a pest <i>Drosophila</i> species that devastates fruit production worldwide. Finally, we summarize recently developed research tools and resources in <i>D. melanogaster</i> that have potential utility for agricultural pest management.","affiliation":"Department of Entomology and Nematology, College of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences, University of California Davis, Davis, CA, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"NIFA","grantId":"CA-D-ENM-2150-H to JCC","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Drosophila","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Drosophila melanogaster","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Insect Control","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Insecticide Resistance","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Drosophila melanogaster","Insecticide resistance","Pest management","Invasive pest","Drosophila Suzukii"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/19336934.2026.2749209"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13690281"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13690281?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-22","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-24","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-21","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-24","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42633213","source":"MED","pmid":"42633213","pmcid":"PMC13499351","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13499351"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.synbio.2026.07.014","title":"Gentamicin and d-serine selective pressure to improve the homogeneity of cells expressing target proteins.","authorString":"Gelain L, Yeoh JW, Au-Yeung BSY, Guillouet S, Poh CL, Gorret N, Foo JL.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Gelain L","lastName":"Gelain","firstName":"Lucas","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"CNRS@CREATE, 1 Create Way, Create Tower, 138602, Singapore."},{"affiliation":"NUS Synthetic Biology for Clinical and Technological Innovation (SynCTI), National University of Singapore, 117456, Singapore."}]}},{"fullName":"Yeoh JW","lastName":"Yeoh","firstName":"Jing Wui","initials":"JW","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"NUS Synthetic Biology for Clinical and Technological Innovation (SynCTI), National University of Singapore, 117456, Singapore."},{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Engineering, National University of Singapore, 117583, Singapore."},{"affiliation":"National Centre for Engineering Biology (NCEB), Singapore."}]}},{"fullName":"Au-Yeung BSY","lastName":"Au-Yeung","firstName":"Benjamin Shang Yong","initials":"BSY","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7904-4254"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"NUS Synthetic Biology for Clinical and Technological Innovation (SynCTI), National University of Singapore, 117456, Singapore."},{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Engineering, National University of Singapore, 117583, Singapore."},{"affiliation":"National Centre for Engineering Biology (NCEB), Singapore."}]}},{"fullName":"Guillouet S","lastName":"Guillouet","firstName":"Stéphane","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"TBI, Université de Toulouse, CNRS, INRAE, INSA, Toulouse, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Poh CL","lastName":"Poh","firstName":"Chueh Loo","initials":"CL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"NUS Synthetic Biology for Clinical and Technological Innovation (SynCTI), National University of Singapore, 117456, Singapore."},{"affiliation":"Department of Biomedical Engineering, National University of Singapore, 117583, Singapore."},{"affiliation":"National Centre for Engineering Biology (NCEB), Singapore."}]}},{"fullName":"Gorret N","lastName":"Gorret","firstName":"Nathalie","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"TBI, Université de Toulouse, CNRS, INRAE, INSA, Toulouse, France."}]}},{"fullName":"Foo JL","lastName":"Foo","firstName":"Jee Loon","initials":"JL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"NUS Synthetic Biology for Clinical and Technological Innovation (SynCTI), National University of Singapore, 117456, Singapore."},{"affiliation":"Synthetic Biology Translational Research Programme, Yong Loo Lin School of Medicine, National University of Singapore, Singapore."},{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry, Yong Loo Lin School of Medicine, National University of Singapore, Singapore."},{"affiliation":"National Centre for Engineering Biology (NCEB), Singapore."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7904-4254"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"17","journalIssueId":4241192,"dateOfPublication":"2027 Mar","monthOfPublication":3,"yearOfPublication":2027,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Synth Syst Biotechnol","title":"Synthetic and systems biotechnology","essn":"2405-805X","issn":"2405-805X","isoabbreviation":"Synth Syst Biotechnol","nlmid":"101694371"},"printPublicationDate":"2027-03-01"},"pubYear":"2027","pageInfo":"144-154","abstractText":"In this work, selective pressure was applied in <i>E</i>. <i>coli</i> to enrich the population expressing recombinant proteins and suppress the emergence of low-expression phenotypes. To this end, plasmids were constructed containing two reporter genes, a degradable green fluorescent protein (GFP) and a stable red fluorescent protein (RFP), positioned upstream of either a gentamicin acetyltransferase gene or a d-serine deaminase gene. Flow cytometry analysis revealed that gentamicin selection prevented the formation of bimodal populations and maintained predominantly homogeneous expression profiles, accompanied by up to 12-fold and 10-fold increases in RFP and GFP fluorescence, respectively. These results suggest that selective pressure favoured the enrichment and maintenance of highly expressing phenotypes while preserving operon functionality. Transcriptomic analysis indicated extensive physiological adaptation under gentamicin selection, including changes in translation-related functions and increased transcript abundance of the genes of interest. To establish an antibiotic-free strategy, d-serine was employed as an alternative selective agent. Detoxification of d-serine by d-serine deaminase similarly reduced population heterogeneity and resulted in predominantly single-expression populations with up to 4-fold and 6-fold higher RFP and GFP fluorescence, respectively. Furthermore, d-serine was evaluated as the sole nitrogen source, combining selective pressure with an auxotrophic strategy and yielding up to 6-fold and 15-fold increases in RFP and GFP fluorescence, respectively. These findings support the use of selective pressure to reduce recombinant expression heterogeneity and suppress low-expression subpopulations. Additionally, we highlighted the potential use of d-serine and similar toxic substrates to simultaneously function as selective agents, inducers, and sources of essential metabolites through detoxification.","affiliation":"CNRS@CREATE, 1 Create Way, Create Tower, 138602, Singapore.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Flow cytometry","D-serine","gentamicin","Population Heterogeneity","Selection System"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.synbio.2026.07.014"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13499351"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13499351?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-23","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-23","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-24","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-10","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42604037","source":"MED","pmid":"42604037","pmcid":"PMC13476631","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13476631"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.mrl.2026.200306","title":"Going high-throughput: Dynamic conformations and interactions &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt;.","authorString":"Gong Z, Zhao Q, Sun M, Zhang B, Zhang X, Zhang L, Zhang Y, Liu M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Gong Z","lastName":"Gong","firstName":"Zhou","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy and Imaging, Innovation Academy for Precision Measurement Science and Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Wuhan, 430071, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhao Q","lastName":"Zhao","firstName":"Qun","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Medical Proteomics, National Chromatographic R. & A. Center, Dalian Institute of Chemical Physics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Dalian, 116023, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Sun M","lastName":"Sun","firstName":"Min","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy and Imaging, Innovation Academy for Precision Measurement Science and Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Wuhan, 430071, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang B","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Beirong","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Medical Proteomics, National Chromatographic R. & A. Center, Dalian Institute of Chemical Physics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Dalian, 116023, China."},{"affiliation":"University of Chinese Academy of Sciences, Beijing, 100049, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang X","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Xu","initials":"X","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy and Imaging, Innovation Academy for Precision Measurement Science and Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Wuhan, 430071, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang L","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Lihua","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Medical Proteomics, National Chromatographic R. & A. Center, Dalian Institute of Chemical Physics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Dalian, 116023, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang Y","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Yukui","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Medical Proteomics, National Chromatographic R. & A. Center, Dalian Institute of Chemical Physics, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Dalian, 116023, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu M","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Maili","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy and Imaging, Innovation Academy for Precision Measurement Science and Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Wuhan, 430071, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"7","journalIssueId":4234501,"dateOfPublication":"2027 Feb","monthOfPublication":2,"yearOfPublication":2027,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Magn Reson Lett","title":"Magnetic resonance letters","essn":"2772-5162","issn":"2097-0048","isoabbreviation":"Magn Reson Lett","nlmid":"9918402274406676"},"printPublicationDate":"2027-02-01"},"pubYear":"2027","pageInfo":"200306","abstractText":"Proteins are intrinsically dynamic molecules that continuously explore conformational ensembles to execute biological functions. Conventional structural biology methods rely on <i>in vitro</i> reconstitution of purified components and therefore capture predominantly static snapshots, often overlooking the regulatory roles of the cellular microenvironment, such as molecular crowding, weak interaction networks, and post-translational modifications. This limitation has driven an urgent need to transition from <i>in vitro</i> reconstruction to <i>in vivo</i> characterization within living cells. Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy provides atomic-resolution insights into structure and motions spanning multiple timescales, yet its application is constrained by molecular weight limits, isotopic labeling requirements, and inherently low throughput. Cross-linking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) complements NMR by delivering sparse but long-range spatial restraints without an upper molecular weight limit. The integration of NMR and XL-MS establishes a powerful synergistic framework that bridges atomic-resolution local structures and large-scale interaction topologies, thereby enabling comprehensive characterization of protein dynamic conformations and interaction networks in native environments. Here, we review how this integrative strategy advances the understanding of intrinsically disordered proteins, multi-domain proteins, and dynamic protein-protein interaction networks in native cellular environments. We further discuss emerging technological frontiers, including hyperpolarized NMR, photo-cross-linking, organelle-resolved analysis, and artificial intelligence-guided integrative modeling, which together promise to transform our ability to resolve the true functional states of proteins inside cells.","affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy and Imaging, Innovation Academy for Precision Measurement Science and Technology, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Wuhan, 430071, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Structure modeling","Protein interaction","In-cell Nmr","Dynamic Conformation","In Vivo Xl-Ms"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mrl.2026.200306"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13476631"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13476631?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["uniprot","pdb","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-16","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-16","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-17","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-04","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-04","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-09","hasEvaluations":"N"}]}}